# PAY — libraries for 402 and related handshakes

File: guide/Pay.oot
User wiki. Canonical URL: https://openooda.org/guide/#Pay.oot

[Home](Home.oot) [Start](Use.oot) [Language](Language.oot) [Commands](Options.oot) [Packages](Packages.oot) [Capabilities](Safety.oot) [Active Defense](Morpher.oot) [How it works](Limits.oot) [Pay](Pay.oot)

These are **standard-library modules**. You `import` them in an `.oo` program if you want that program to speak HTTP 402, x402 v2, or the neighbor handshakes (capability quote, hashcash, inverted offer, 425, Privacy Pass).

They parse and format quotes. They do not open a wallet, send USDC, or settle a chain payment. Your program still has to do HTTP, and if you sell, your server still has to send 402.

Spec (x402 v2): https://github.com/x402-foundation/x402/blob/main/specs/x402-specification-v2.md
HTTP transport: https://github.com/x402-foundation/x402/blob/main/specs/transports-v2/http.md

## 1. What you get

HTTP 402 means payment required.
x402 v2 is a handshake on that status: the server quotes a price, the client may attach a signed payload and retry.

Import the helpers, read the quote, print it, and (if you want) build a 402 body for your own server.

The public door does not charge.
A tip quote Worker is `GET https://pay.openooda.org/tip` — HTTP 402 plus a `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` header.
Authors put their own `payTo` on a package manifest. `ooda add` may print that a price is listed. It still downloads. It does not charge.

v2 headers, when you implement the HTTP side:

| Header | Direction | Contents |
|---|---|---|
| `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` | server → client | base64 `PaymentRequired` JSON |
| `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE` | client → server | base64 `PaymentPayload` JSON |
| `PAYMENT-RESPONSE` | server → client | base64 settlement JSON |

This library does not send `PAYMENT-RESPONSE`. That header is a settlement receipt.

## 2. Import x402

```
import "std/net/x402.oo";
import "std/net/x402_spend.oo";
```

These files are on `main`.

## 3. See a 402

`x402_is_payment_required(status)` is true only for `402`.
`http_response_reason(402)` is `Payment Required`.

Decode the `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` header to JSON, then parse:

```
let q: X402Quote = x402_parse_quote(body);
if x402_is_payment_required(402) {
    if x402_quote_ready(q) {
        println(x402_format_quote(q));
    }
}
```

`x402_quote_ready` is true when `payTo`, `amount`, `network`, `asset`, and `resource.url` are present.
`amount` is atomic token units. `network` is CAIP-2 (`eip155:8453` is Base).
`x402_format_quote` prints one line: amount, network, pay-to address.

A fixture you can parse without the network: `fixtures/x402_quote.json`.
Check: `ooda run scripts/x402_parse_smoke.oo`.

## 4. Build a v2 quote (if you sell)

If your program is the seller, build the JSON, then put it in `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` (base64) on HTTP 402:

```
let body: String = x402_required_json(
    "0xYourAddress",
    "1000",
    "eip155:8453",
    "0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913",
    "https://example.com/resource"
);
```

That is a v2 `PaymentRequired` string. You still send HTTP 402 from your server.
GitHub Pages cannot do that handshake. A Worker or other origin can.

## 5. Attaching a proof is opt-in

`x402_attach_or_deny` is a guard your program calls **before** it puts `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE` on a request.

You pass two strings: a flag, and the proof. The flag must be exactly `1`, and the proof must not be empty. Otherwise you get `Err`. Callers usually pass `OODA_X402_SPEND` as that flag. If you pass `""`, attach is refused.

```
let pin: String = "";
let r: Result[Bool, String] = x402_attach_or_deny(pin, "proof");
if r.is_err() {
    println("spend denied");
}
```

`Ok(true)` means “your program may attach the header.” It is not a payment. There is no chain send in this library.

The same idea is used on the other modules, each with its own flag name (`OODA_CAP_SPEND`, `OODA_WORK_SPEND`, `OODA_OFFER_CLAIM`, `OODA_EARLY_RETRY`, `OODA_PASS_ATTACH`). Default is refuse. Your program chooses when to pass `1`.

## 6. Pay with a capability

A 402 can ask for a capability name (for example `FsRead`) instead of USDC. Import this if you want that quote in your program.

```
import "std/net/pay_cap.oo";

let q: PayCapQuote = pay_cap_parse(body);
if pay_cap_ready(q) {
    println(pay_cap_format(q));
}
```

Headers: `CAP-REQUIRED`, `CAP-PROOF`.
`pay_cap_ready` needs `scheme=cap`, a cap name, and `resource.url`.
This module does not check a live capability token.

## 7. Pay with work

A 402 can ask for hashcash (SHA-256 leading zero bits). Import this to parse the quote and check a stamp.

```
import "std/net/pay_work.oo";

let q: PayWorkQuote = pay_work_parse(body);
if pay_work_stamp_ok(stamp, q.bits, q.resource) {
    println(pay_work_format(q));
}
```

Headers: `WORK-REQUIRED`, `WORK-PROOF`.
Stamp shape: `ver:bits:date:resource:ext:rand:counter`.
The library verifies a stamp. It does not mint one.

## 8. Inverted 402

The server offers to pay the client. That is not an IETF status.
Use HTTP 200 plus `PAYMENT-OFFERED`.

```
import "std/net/pay_offer.oo";

let o: PayOffer = pay_offer_parse(body);
if pay_offer_ready(o) {
    println(pay_offer_format(o));
}
```

Parsing an offer is not collecting money.

## 9. 425 Too Early

HTTP 425 means the proof arrived before it was valid (RFC 8470).
`http_response_reason(425)` is `Too Early`.
`pay_early_is_too_early(425)` is true. 402 is not 425.

```
import "std/net/pay_early.oo";

let e: PayEarly = pay_early_parse(body);
```

`pay_early_ready` needs `validAfter`. This module does not read a clock.

## 10. Privacy Pass

Scrape a `PrivateToken` challenge (RFC 9577 shape). Import this to read the challenge. It does not mint or redeem tokens.

```
import "std/net/pay_pass.oo";

let p: PayPass = pay_pass_parse(header);
```

Ready when `challenge` and `token-key` are both present.

Check all five neighbor modules: `ooda run scripts/pay_handshakes_smoke.oo`.

Capabilities for network fetch are on [Capabilities](Safety.oot).
First run is on [Start](Use.oot).
